==English==
{{was wotd|2024|November|13}}

===Etymology===
{{uncertain|en|Origin uncertain}}; according to the American etymologist and linguist {{w|Anatoly Liberman}} (born 1937), probably a nautical term drawing an analogy between paying a large sum of money and [[pay out|paying out]] an [[anchor#Noun|anchor]]’s cable or chain through the [[hawsehole]]s at the [[bow#Noun|bow]] (metaphorically the “nose”) of a ship.<ref>{{cite-book|author=w:Anatoly Liberman|chapter=A Few Idioms|title=Origin Uncertain: Unraveling the Mysteries of Etymology|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Oxford University Press|month=February|year=2024|pages=302–303|doi=10.1093/oso/9780197664919.003.0014|isbn=978-0-19-766494-0}} (also at {{cite-web|author=Anatoly Liberman|title=Why Pay through the Nose?|work=[[w:Oxford University Press|OUPblog]]|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20240205062853/https://blog.oup.com/2010/10/pay-through-the-nose/|date=13 October 2010}}), quoting {{cite-journal|author=Richard Edgcumbe|title=Horse-Marine|journal=[[w:Notes and Queries|Notes and Queries: A Medium of Inter-communication for Literary Men, General Readers, etc.]]|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed by}} John Edward Francis,{{nb...|Athenæum Press, Bream’s Buildings, Chancery Lane, E.C.; and published by}} [for] [[w:John Francis (publisher)|John C. Francis]]{{nb...|at Bream’s Buildings, Chancery Lane, E.C.}}|date=3 December 1898|volume=II (9th Series)|issue=49|pages=456–457|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sim_notes-and-queries_1898-12-03_2_49/page/457/mode/1up|oclc=611217138|passage=Then, again, “Paying through the nose.” This was originally a common expression on board ship: “Pay out the cable,” “pay out handsomely.” The nose of a ship is, of course, the bow; its nostrils are the hawse holes on either side. Now, it does not seem very difficult (at all events, for a sailor) to associate extortionate disbursements with handsome payments—such, for instance, as paying out a chain cable (through the nose), especially when the order is conveyed in such language as this, “Pay out handsomely.” At all events, I can speak on this matter from personal experience as a midshipman. To my mind “paying through the nose” for anything has always been associated with the rattling of a “payed out” chain cable, after the anchor has gripped the ground.}}</ref> He is unconvinced of other explanations such as the following:<ref>Liberman, pages 301–302.</ref>

* The term is said to derive from the story of the Norse god [[Odin]] levying a tax on the nose of every Swede. However, Liberman is of the view that it is unclear why a god would require money.
* Alternatively, the term is said to be from {{noncog|non|nef-gildi|lit=nose-tax|t=[[poll tax]] payable to the king}}, ''nose'' being a {{glossary|synecdoche}} referring to a person, because the Irish were required to pay such a tax to the Vikings who conquered them (795–1169 {{C.E.}}). Liberman points out that the English term is only attested centuries after this period.

